.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt MQ_OPEN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mq_open ,
.Nm mq_unlink ,
.Nm mq_timedsend ,
.Nm mq_timedreceive ,
.Nm mq_notify ,
.Nm mq_getsetattr
.Nd POSIX message queues
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In mqueue.h
.Ft mqd_t
.Fn mq_open "const char *name" "int oflag" "..."
.Ft int
.Fn mq_unlink "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn mq_timedsend "mqd_t mqdes" "const char *msg" "size_t len" "unsigned prio" "const struct timespec *abs"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn mq_timedreceive "mqd_t mqdes" "char *msg" "size_t len" "unsigned *prio" "const struct timespec *abs"
.Ft int
.Fn mq_notify "mqd_t mqdes" "const struct sigevent *sevp"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A queue has a name, which every process shares, and messages of at most
its message size, at most its count of them, kept highest priority first
and in the order they came within a priority.
The C library passes the name without its leading slash, and calls the
attribute calls through
.Fn mq_getsetattr .
A queue lives while its name or a descriptor has it.
.Pp
.Fn mq_open
returns a descriptor open on the queue
.Fa name
for reading, writing or both, with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC
as
.Fa oflag
gives them.
With
.Dv O_CREAT
it makes the queue if there is none, the caller's, with the mode its
umask leaves, and the count and size of the
.Vt struct mq_attr
given, at most 10 and 8192 for an unprivileged caller, or 10 and 8192
without one.
An unprivileged caller may make 256 queues.
.Pp
.Fn mq_unlink
takes the name away from a queue the caller owns, or any for a
privileged caller.
.Pp
.Fn mq_timedsend
puts a message of priority
.Fa prio ,
below 32768, waiting while the queue is full until the absolute
real-time
.Fa abs ,
or not at all for a descriptor with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
.Fn mq_timedreceive
takes the first message and its priority, waiting while the queue is
empty.
.Pp
.Fn mq_notify
registers the caller for the next message that reaches the queue empty
while no receiver waits:
.Dv SIGEV_SIGNAL
posts the signal asked for, with
.Dv SI_MESGQ ,
the value given, and the sender's pid and uid, and
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
posts nothing; the registration goes either way, and with the
registered process's last close of the queue.
A NULL
.Fa sevp
takes the caller's registration away.
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD ,
which Linux carries through a netlink socket of a kind this kernel does
not have, is
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
.Xr poll 2
reports a descriptor readable while the queue has a message and writable
while it has room, and a
.Xr read 2
of it gives Linux's line of the queued bytes and the registration:
.Dl QSIZE:N NOTIFY:N SIGNO:N NOTIFY_PID:N
There is no
.Pa /dev/mqueue .
.Ss System call interface
The numbers are 277 to 282 in the order above, with the timeouts as
32-bit timespecs, and 418
.Pq Fn mq_timedsend_time64
and 419
.Pq Fn mq_timedreceive_time64
with 64-bit seconds.
.Fn mq_getsetattr
copies the attributes out, the flags
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
or 0, then takes
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
alone from the new ones.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn mq_open
returns a descriptor,
.Fn mq_timedreceive
the message's length, and the others 0; on failure -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
A name with a slash, an open its mode does not give, or an unlink of
another user's queue.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A full queue to send to or an empty one to receive from, with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
.It Bq Er EBADF
A descriptor not open on a queue, or not open for the transfer.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Another process is registered.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
.Dv O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_EXCL
for a queue that exists.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal ended the wait.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An access mode of 3, attributes outside the caller's limits, a priority
past 32767, a malformed timeout, a notification of another kind or a
signal outside 1 to 64, or a flag besides
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
to set.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
A message past the queue's size, or a buffer smaller than it.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A name past 255 bytes.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
256 queues already, for an unprivileged caller.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
No such queue and no
.Dv O_CREAT ,
or an empty name.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
The timeout ran out.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr msgget 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
